WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.584
Not all iterators support the same operations.

2
00:00:02.584 --> 00:00:04.799
Some are single-pass input iterators.

3
00:00:04.799 --> 00:00:08.121
Some are forward iterators that support multi-pass traversal.

4
00:00:08.121 --> 00:00:09.228
Some are bidirectional.

5
00:00:09.228 --> 00:00:11.443
Some are random-access or contiguous.

6
00:00:11.443 --> 00:00:18.087
The operations that are safe and efficient depend on the category and on the range behind the iterator.

7
00:00:18.087 --> 00:00:22.516
A common mistake is to treat every iterator like a vector iterator.

8
00:00:22.516 --> 00:00:30.268
Subtraction, indexing, jumping forward by an arbitrary distance, or assuming constant-time movement may be valid for random-access iterators and

9
00:00:30.268 --> 00:00:32.114
invalid or expensive for others.

10
00:00:32.114 --> 00:00:38.389
Repeated traversal may be safe for a forward range and unsafe for a single-pass input range.

11
00:00:38.389 --> 00:00:41.342
Generic code should express its iterator requirements clearly.

12
00:00:41.342 --> 00:00:48.355
In modern C++, concepts can state that an algorithm needs a forward iterator, random-access range, or contiguous range.

13
00:00:48.355 --> 00:00:54.261
In older code, template structure, function names, documentation, assertions, and tests can still make requirements visible.

14
00:00:54.261 --> 00:00:55.738
Clear constraints protect callers.

15
00:00:55.738 --> 00:01:04.596
If a function accepts any iterator but internally assumes random access, the problem may not appear until a different container or range is used.

16
00:01:04.596 --> 00:01:07.919
That is a maintainability hazard and a correctness risk.

17
00:01:07.919 --> 00:01:11.979
Defensive APIs make the required traversal behavior part of the contract.

18
00:01:11.979 --> 00:01:14.563
Iterator category awareness also helps performance review.

19
00:01:14.563 --> 00:01:19.731
Code that repeatedly advances through a linked structure may be correct but unexpectedly costly.

20
00:01:19.731 --> 00:01:24.160
Safety and performance both improve when traversal assumptions match the actual range.
